  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-11-03T09:20:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "5acc2084-192c-4ed5-89a1-ee6b5bba46db",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 030920\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n220 AM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Flooding of several area streams and rivers continues to\n  subside, while major waterways like the Salt River and Gila\n  River will likely continue to experience flooding through the \n  week due to dam releases.\n \n- Dry weather with seasonable temperatures will continue the next\n  several days with lower desert highs warming into upper 70s to \n  middle 80s by the end of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \n\nA considerable volume of water continues to flow downhill through\nmany of the major waterways in south-central AZ early this\nmorning, especially in the Salt and Verde systems. Salt River\nProject releases from the Bartlett Dam into the Verde River and\nGranite Reef Dam into the Salt River, remain very high, over 30000\ncfs and over 55000 cfs respectively. Streamflow gauges were also\nreading between 30000-60000 cfs downstream from Tempe Town Lake \nheading into the Gila River. The last time there were major \nreleases this high was in the Spring of 2023 and the Granite Reef\nDam releases peaked at just under 40000 cfs and the runoff lasted\nfor 2-3 months before fully emptying into the Colorado River near\nYuma. That event in 2023 involved snow melt. So, there was a more\nprolonged runoff, but this event has a larger volume of water. \nWater levels and flow of this magnitude will continue to impact \nseveral low water crossings and any settlements, structures, and \ncamps immediately near the banks. Very strong currents will also \nbe very dangerous and deadly. There have been multiple fatalities \nby people recreating in the Salt and Verde with past events. So, \nit is strongly encouraged to avoid entering the water and please \nobey any closures. Also, be prepared for significant flood water \ncontinuing through the Gila River to Painted Rock Dam in Maricopa \nCounty. From there it may take a few days before water is released\nfrom Painted Rock into the lower Gila in Yuma County.\n\nWith clear skies and very light winds this morning, and with the \nlingering saturated grounds, there is potential for some patchy \nfog to develop this morning. Many areas are just too dry for\ndevelopment, but following cross-over temperature analysis, the \nbest shot at fog development looks to be in parts of Pinal \nCounty, along the Gila River in Maricopa and Yuma Counties, and \nparts of eastern La Paz County. \n\nBesides the flooding and potential fog, weather will remain dry \nand pleasant through the middle of the week. Water vapor satellite\nthis morning shows shortwave ridging moving over the area ahead of\na deep Pacific low just off the NorCal coast. Model guidance\nremains in excellent agreement showing this Pacific low tracking\nthrough NV, UT, and CO today through tomorrow, staying far enough\nnorth to have minimal impact on the local weather, besides\nsuppressing heights again following the shortwave ridge.\nComfortable afternoon temperatures are expected each day, with \nhighs in the 70s to near 80, and mornings will start out\nseasonally cool, mostly in the low to mid 50s across the lower\ndeserts to mid to upper 40s in the typically cooler spots.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \n\nThe long term forecast remains dry and seasonal. Ensemble guidance\ncontinues to show a dry positively-tilted trough dropping through\nthe area at the end of the week, ahead of a strong ridge spreading\ninto much of the west. Guidance also shows a cutoff low forming\nsouth of the ridge over the eastern Pacific, west of the Baja\nPeninsula. From here the models diverge a bit. A majority of the\nGEFS members bring the cutoff low up into the Desert Southwest,\ndrawing some moisture up with it. However, the rest of the global\nensemble guidance leans toward a much weaker cutoff low, with the\nDesert Southwest remaining dominated by ridging. Latest NBM PoPs\nthrough the long term forecast remain under 5% across the local\narea. Temperatures late-week through this weekend should warm a\nfew degrees, back to around normal as the ridge builds over the\nWest. Lower desert highs are forecast to be in the upper 70s to\nlower 80s. Breezy northerly winds are likely to develop heading\ninto the end of this week and weekend as surface high pressure\nbuilds over the Great Basin and Rockies, below the upper level\nridge, but wind magnitudes do not look to reach advisory level.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0505Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; and\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major weather concerns will exist through Tuesday evening under \nperiods of passing high cirrus. Confidence is good that light east \nwinds will develop across the Phoenix area through Tuesday afternoon \nbefore attempting to become W/NW by sunset. However, directions may \nonly become variable with speeds hovering under 8kt before \nreverting back to easterly. Across SE California, light W/NW winds \nwill be preferred through the period with prolonged periods of light \nand variable winds common.  \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry seasonal conditions can be expected through the next 7 days,\nwith lingering flooding in major waterways like the Salt and Gila\nRivers. Saturated grounds will help keep surface humidity levels \nsomewhat elevated. Afternoon minimum RHs today will be around \n40-55% across AZ and 20-30% across southeast CA. Despite gradual \ndrying through the week, the minimum RHs will only fall to around \n30-40% across AZ and 17-25% across southeast CA by the end of the\nweek. Overnight recoveries will remain good through the week, \nwith around 60-80% max RHs most nights. Winds through the next 7 \ndays will mostly follow typical diurnal trends with light speeds \nand modest afternoon upslope gusts. Breezier north to northeast \nwinds are anticipated late week into this weekend with gusts up \nto 20-30 mph forecast at this time.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict \nLONG TERM...Benedict \nAVIATION...18 \nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict\n",
